 "notes": "Sensitivity: anomaly over days 0-13, -0.0112 (-0.0224 to +0.0000); days 14-27, +0.0018; cases instead of admissions, -0.0012; dropping 2020, +0.0030; dropping the Omicron weeks, +0.0037. All intervals include 0; the days 0-13 window is the only negative one and too short for an infection to reach hospital. The raw quarterly Spearman correlations of daily temperature with daily cases range from -0.79 (2020 Q2) to +0.77 (2024 Q2). Exploratory: over the first 30, 45, 60 and 90 days from 1 March 2020 the raw correlation is +0.03, +0.17, +0.14 and -0.22.\n\nAfter the referee (13 Nov 2026): the end of the file is incomplete and the registered filter did not protect against it. The last five days report 4, 2, 1, 1 and 0 admissions against about 20 a day before, and the week ending 12 October 2025 passes the filter of 20 with the most negative growth in the sample (-0.75). Dropping the last three weeks gives +0.0078 (-0.0009 to +0.0165); the verdict does not change. The sign is not stable: weighting weeks by the precision of the log ratio gives -0.0025 (-0.0129 to +0.0079), and single-week exposure windows alternate in sign, as serial dependence in weekly growth would produce. With five or six large waves, a chance alignment of two or three wave onsets with warm or cold spells can move a coefficient of this size, and year effects with harmonics do not remove it. Care-seeking and admission practice respond to weather within the same week and were not named as an alternative. The lower limit (-0.0036) lies just inside the registered range, so the weakest registered effect is not excluded; the earlier wording 'excludes effects more negative than -0.004' should be read with that. On the graph side, PMID 33162141 reports a negative coefficient for Bangladesh and a positive one for Afghanistan and describes mixed effects, so 'mixed' is the accurate code for that study.",
